{{nihongo|'''Honda Shigetoki'''|本多重時}} (14 January 1583 – 12 November 1651) was the third [[shogun]] of the Honda Shogunate in Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). He was the younger half-brother of the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], and the son of the founder of the shogunate, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]]. Shigetoki's reign was defined by his sudden ascension to power during the most volatile crisis of the early Honda period, assuming control of the nation after his father and half-brother were killed within weeks of each other during the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625.

Despite immense pressure from loyalist daimyos to completely eradicate the [[Ii clan]] for their role in the Emperor's assassination and the Shogun's death, Shigetoki made the highly controversial but ultimately stabilizing decision to grant them amnesty. This act, known as the Great Reconciliation, prevented a second Sengoku period and secured over two centuries of relative peace under Honda rule.

==Early life==
Shigetoki was born in early 1583 in Suemori, just three months after his older half-brother Shigetomo. Unlike Shigetomo, who was born to Shigetada's primary wife [[Hatsu-no-kata]] (the daughter of the revered [[Anjō Motonobu]]), Shigetoki was born to a low-ranking concubine named Lady Okyō. His childhood name was {{nihongo|Naimon|内門}}. Because he lacked the prestigious Anjō maternal lineage, Shigetoki was never considered the primary candidate for the succession of the shogunate.

While Shigetomo received rigorous martial training and was groomed for supreme command alongside their father, earning the nickname "Punisher," Shigetoki was given a quieter, scholarly upbringing. He was tutored by Neo-Confucian scholars and heavily exposed to the bureaucratic and logistical mechanisms of the new government in Okami. He spent much of his early adulthood observing the implementation of the Hokuto Omi - Shinshu road construction and assisting in the management of the Okami Rice Exchange. This administrative background made him a highly competent bureaucrat, preparing him for a crisis no one expected him to lead. 

==The Kinzō Crisis of 1625==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}
The political landscape of Mitoyagi fractured in the summer of 1625. On 6 June, a coalition of disgruntled vassals led by {{nihongo|[[Ii Naotaka]]|井伊直孝}} attacked the retirement palace at Toyozawa, resulting in the death of Shigetoki's father, the Retired Emperor Shirakawa. 

Driven by grief and rage, Shogun Shigetomo launched a massive, erratic retaliatory campaign against the Ii clan at [[Ichijoudani]]. On 18 June 1625, Shigetomo was mortally wounded outside the castle gates by an Ii musketeer. Within the span of twelve days, the Honda Shogunate had lost its two most powerful figureheads. 

Shigetoki, who had originally been ordered by Shigetomo to move his soldiers 25 miles north to open a new front, was suddenly thrust into supreme command. At 42 years old, the scholarly half-brother found himself the de facto leader of the military government. The situation was catastrophic: the imperial court was in shock, the main Honda armies were exhausted and leaderless at Ichijoudani, and several tozama daimyos were already preparing to rebel, sensing the imminent collapse of the Honda hegemony.

==The Great Reconciliation==
{{Main|Battle of Ichijoudani}}
The immediate expectation among the Honda retainers, particularly those of the [[Hotta clan]] who had fought beside Shigetomo, was absolute vengeance. The council of elders demanded that Shigetoki mobilize the remaining reserves, march on Ichijoudani, and execute every man, woman, and child bearing the Ii name.

Shigetoki recognized that giving into this bloodlust would spark a nationwide civil war. The Ii clan still commanded immense loyalty, and annihilating them would require years of grinding warfare that the fragile, post-unification economy could not sustain. His decision was heavily influenced by the recovery of the bloodstained "Ii Letters"—the final correspondence found on his brother's body. In these letters, Shigetomo had expressed profound regret over his own erratic actions and a dying wish to forgive Naotaka to preserve the peace.

In late July 1625, Shigetoki shocked the nation by issuing the Edict of Clemency. Instead of ordering the destruction of the Ii clan, Shigetoki officially pardoned them. He declared that the deaths of his father and brother were tragic consequences of political misunderstandings rather than malicious treason. He negotiated a settlement wherein the Ii clan was allowed to keep their territory in Ichijoudani, though they were stripped of 70,000 koku to pay for war reparations and the construction of memorial temples.

This act of profound pragmatism, later dubbed the Great Reconciliation, successfully defused the crisis. By absorbing the emotional blow of his family's deaths for the sake of the state, Shigetoki won the deep, enduring respect of both the fudai and tozama daimyos. The Ii clan, indebted to Shigetoki's mercy, became the most staunchly loyal defenders of the Honda Shogunate for the next two centuries.

==Reign as Shogun==
Following the resolution of the Kinzō crisis, Shigetoki was officially invested as the third Shogun on 10 July 1625 by his brother, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]]. 

Shigetoki's twenty-six-year tenure was characterized by a shift from military expansion to bureaucratic consolidation. Recognizing that his father and brother had ruled through sheer force of personality and martial terror, Shigetoki sought to institutionalize the shogunate's power so it could survive weaker rulers. 

He slowed the aggressive implementation of the Kinzō reforms that had triggered the initial rebellion, opting for a gradual transition of land rights that appeased the conservative samurai class. He also expanded the Okami Rice Exchange and heavily subsidized the arts, leading to a flourishing of early modern Sanese culture. He strictly maintained the separation of the Imperial Court and the military government, ensuring that Emperor Daigo remained entirely focused on ceremonial and religious duties while the bakufu handled statecraft.

