==Death and Legacy==
Shigetoki died peacefully of natural causes at Okami Castle on 12 November 1651 at the age of 68. He was succeeded by Shigetomo's eldest son, [[Honda Shigemasa]], seamlessly passing power back to the primary lineage. Shigetoki had previously adopted Shigetomo's second son, Honda Tadamasa, to ensure his own branch family's continuation.

While he is rarely depicted in historical dramas with the same dramatic flair as his legendary father or his volatile half-brother, historians widely consider Shigetoki to be the savior of the Honda Shogunate. Without his cool-headed intervention and his willingness to forego personal vengeance, the unified Mitoyagi state would almost certainly have collapsed back into the warring chaos of the Sengoku period.

==In Popular Culture==
Shigetoki frequently appears in media dealing with the Kinzō Incident, usually portrayed as the voice of reason contrasting with Shigetomo's rage. In the acclaimed Taisen Drama ''Honda Shigetomo'' (1983), he was portrayed as a deeply conflicted man forced to carry the weight of an empire. He also plays a major supporting role in the 2008 series ''Genna Revolt'', which focuses on the political challenges of transitioning from a wartime to a peacetime government.

